  <section id="faq">
    <h2>What else do people ask about failed AI projects?</h2>

    <h3>Why do most AI projects fail?</h3>
    <p>Mostly because the business problem was never pinned down, or the process was broken before
       the AI arrived. RAND names a misunderstood problem as the most common cause, and BCG found
       that the leading companies put about 70% of their resources into people and processes. The technology
       is rarely the main reason.</p>

    <h3>What is the AI project failure rate?</h3>
    <p>RAND cites estimates of more than 80%, about twice the rate of other IT projects, while
       noting that these are estimates. In S&amp;P Global's 2025 survey, reported by CIO Dive, 42%
       of businesses were scrapping most of their AI initiatives. The exact figure depends on how
       each study defines failure.</p>

    <h3>Why do AI pilots fail to reach daily use?</h3>
    <p>A pilot usually runs on clean sample data with a keen team watching it. Daily work brings
       exceptions, messy inputs and busy staff who have no time to check the output. S&amp;P
       Global's survey, reported by CIO Dive, found the average organisation scrapped 46% of its
       proofs of concept before production.</p>

    <h3>How do you make an AI project succeed?</h3>
    <p>Start with one clearly written business problem, time how the work is done today, and fix
       exceptions, ownership and data first. Then choose the tool that fits the work, and decide
       who checks its output. The steps above, taken from RAND's recommendations, are a good
       checklist.</p>

    <h3>Do you sell or build the AI tools you recommend?</h3>
    <p>No. We do not sell, resell or build any AI product, software or hardware, and we take no
       commissions from providers. If we name a product, you buy it directly from its maker. More
       answers are in the <a href="/consulting/#questions">questions about our assessment</a>.</p>
  </section>
</Learn>
